<Base title="Why I made this" description="The first trip with our son, and why TinyTrip exists.">
  <article class="inner narrow">
    <p class="page-eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1 class="page-title">Why I made this</h1>

    <div class="why-body">
      <p>
        When our son was 4 months old, we took him on his first trip: a resort in Portugal.
      </p>
      <p>
        Everything was provided. And I still had no idea how to plan for it, or whether it was going to be okay.
      </p>
      <p>
        The trips after that got easier. But getting ready never stopped being mentally hard, because each trip
        brought something I’d never faced before. What do we do if he gets sick the day we arrive? How do you deal
        with a bag of dirty clothes in public, with nowhere to wash them?
      </p>
      <p>
        TinyTrip is for new parents asking the question I did: <em>can we actually do this?</em> It’s a simple
        place to work out whether your baby is ready, what to pack, how travel day might go, and what to do when
        things don’t go to plan.
      </p>

      <h2>What it is, and what it isn’t</h2>
      <p>
        It gives advice. It isn’t a source of truth. Health and travel points link to the official pages they
        come from, like the NHS and GOV.UK, and anything marked “still being double-checked” is waiting for a
        final review. Your GP, health visitor or pharmacist knows your baby best.
      </p>

      <h2>How it’s made</h2>
      <p>
        It’s a small independent project. I built it with Claude Code, an AI coding tool, and wrote the advice
        with its help, checking health and travel points against official sources. Nothing is sold here. Your trip
        details are saved in your own browser, not on a server, unless you share a link or send feedback.
        To see whether the site is helping anyone, I count visits with Cloudflare Web Analytics, which doesn’t
        use cookies or collect personal information.
      </p>

      <h2>Help make it better</h2>
      <p>
        It’s still early, and it gets better with every parent who uses it. One parent’s feedback has already
        reshaped the packing list: items that do several jobs, and what you can leave at home. If something is
        missing, confusing or still worrying you, the three questions below come straight to me.
      </p>
      <p class="why-sign">DK</p>
    </div>

    <div class="next-step">
      <a class="btn-coral" href="/ready">Start with: is baby ready? →</a>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

